# Musical Interaction Pattern

This is a musical application. All sound-triggering interactions must follow musical instrument conventions:

## Core Principle

**Mouse/touch DOWN = sound starts immediately**
**Mouse/touch UP = sound stops**

This mirrors how real instruments work - you don't wait for key release to hear a piano note.

## Implementation

Use `DragGesture(minimumDistance: 0)` instead of `Button` or `onTapGesture`:

```swift
// ❌ BAD - triggers on release
Button(action: { playSound() }) {
    Text("Play")
}

// ❌ BAD - triggers on release
.onTapGesture { playSound() }

// ✅ GOOD - triggers on press, stops on release
.gesture(
    DragGesture(minimumDistance: 0)
        .onChanged { _ in
            if !isPlaying {
                startSound()
            }
        }
        .onEnded { _ in
            stopSound()
        }
)
```

## Guard Against Re-triggering

The `onChanged` handler fires continuously during drag. Always guard against re-triggering:

```swift
.onChanged { _ in
    if !isPlaying {  // Only trigger once
        startSound()
    }
}
```

## Applies To

- Piano keyboard keys
- Sound pad buttons
- Phoneme/syllable segments in sequencer
- Any UI element that produces sound
